<p>
  A <strong>segment</strong> is a stretch of road or trail you want to compare
  yourself on: a climb, a sprint, a lap of the park. Each time one of your
  workouts covers it, Allure records an <strong>effort</strong>. Alongside
  segments there are <strong>routes</strong> (a whole course to follow) and
  <strong>loops</strong> (a course returning to its start).
</p>

<h2>Suggested routes</h2>
<p>
  When you have done the same complete route at least three times without
  saving it, Allure suggests it on each of those activities. « Save as route »
  opens the route form: the activities of the group will be attached to it.
  « Dismiss » closes the suggestion for that route, and later repeats join it
  silently. A suggestion is never published.
</p>

<h2>Who sees a segment</h2>
<p>
  Whoever creates it chooses: only themselves, their followers, or everyone on
  this instance. A private segment has no leaderboard; it only collects your own
  efforts. Anyone who can see a shared segment can copy it into their own
  private segments, in either direction.
</p>

<h2>Who sees your efforts</h2>
<p>
  On a segment's page you only ever see your own efforts in full. The
  <strong>leaderboard</strong> is different: it follows its reader.
</p>
<div class="alert alert-info" role="note">
  <%= t("segments.listing.notice") %>
</div>
<p>
  So two people may see different leaderboards, and an unpublished workout, or
  one published without its route, never puts you on one. On every segment where
  you have an effort, a line tells you who sees you there — or why nobody does.
</p>
<div class="alert alert-info" role="note">
  <%= t("segments.leaderboard.residual_risk") %>
</div>

<h2>Leaving a leaderboard</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Leave the leaderboard</strong>, on a segment's page, removes your
    efforts from it at once, whatever their publication. You can come back
    later.</li>
  <li>A segment touched by one of your privacy zones never shows your
    efforts.</li>
  <li>Leaderboards never leave this instance.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Categories</h2>
<p>
  In your <%= link_to "profile settings", settings_profile_path %> you may
  declare a leaderboard category (Women or Men). It is optional and adds a
  filtered view to leaderboards that have enough athletes in it; you always stay
  on the general leaderboard. Anyone reading a leaderboard you are on can see
  the category you declared, even under an alias.
</p>

<h2>Your own places</h2>
<p>
  The trophy marks your own places — your record and your second and third best
  times, against yourself. The laurel wreath marks a place on a leaderboard,
  against others. Your second and third best times are shown to you only.
</p>
